#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124323 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4940fa22-1584-565f-81fe-dc12e5132f23" :question/math-academy-id "e-8294" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\dfrac12x^2+y^2,$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124324 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0246ee6-702f-5d8d-8482-8db1c46b32ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-8295" :question/problem "Given that $w(r, \\theta)=r \\cos 3\\theta,$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial r}\\cos\\theta - \\dfrac{1}{r}\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta }=-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124325 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ee45610-e14c-593e-9002-e2b2fcdb4517" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1e69bbe-b079-56e5-9ce0-296785d73100" :question/math-academy-id "e-8296" :question/problem "Given that $w(x,y,z)=z^3(x^2+2y^2),$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r, \\theta,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta, y=r\\sin\\theta,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂w}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂w}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=2x{z}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=4y{z}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r, \\theta,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =2r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =4r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =-(2r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(4r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-2{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366747 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c06393f-3c6e-550b-ae5d-be0bc7099075" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366749} {:answer/id #uuid "127ac0c1-1796-5cdc-aa80-a3e74c3f42fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366750} {:answer/id #uuid "2f42f65a-52b4-56bf-a373-d56604c03f4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366751} {:answer/id #uuid "12637f1d-77e2-5692-b025-b04100bdd49a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366752} {:answer/id #uuid "e979cf36-049d-5900-9b6c-1ecb030ce7c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c06393f-3c6e-550b-ae5d-be0bc7099075" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366749} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f65549c-eab0-5032-9eca-43a375b5fa09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88fe0e29-ee8f-57ea-b3f1-0e9eed66bee2" :question/math-academy-id "q-165282" :question/problem "Given that $h(r,\\theta )=r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1),$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂y},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂h}{∂y}=\\frac{∂h}{∂r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ee45610-e14c-593e-9002-e2b2fcdb4517" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d03f6b2-1bf3-5896-b6db-efa0d4b1f9bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2rz\\sin^{2} \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123977} {:answer/id #uuid "390b612c-c68a-575e-a80f-974a20f1431d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2rz\\cos \\theta  + r^{2}\\sin \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123978} {:answer/id #uuid "3e4cb3c9-268e-56d6-9db2-a8ab8e396364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r^{2}\\sin 2\\theta  - r\\cos \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123979} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3b9a2f-8b5f-5200-9cf6-c9e6d4e425b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r^{2}\\sin \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123980} {:answer/id #uuid "7144ba48-e3c0-53eb-83f0-1f3ab6763675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2rz\\cos \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7144ba48-e3c0-53eb-83f0-1f3ab6763675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2rz\\cos \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123981} :answer-field/id #uuid "166da9fd-7349-5b3a-bb26-b20f451ef463" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6edb4185-988d-5465-a73c-d87078eb5744" :question/math-academy-id "q-94770" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2yz,$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y+2z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2z)r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366754 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "165275f1-de5a-53e6-82ab-f98c29570439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366756} {:answer/id #uuid "200e20ad-d870-5801-bdf5-364afee10d96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366757} {:answer/id #uuid "f4838bac-0e01-55a0-9aef-ccb781e858d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366758} {:answer/id #uuid "dd77d566-81a3-5ec5-804d-fe7535017740" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366759} {:answer/id #uuid "e0712546-b360-5085-9b44-f7ec919dd008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "200e20ad-d870-5801-bdf5-364afee10d96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366757} :answer-field/id #uuid "72a9a7b6-c213-5edf-b6fc-f1c5eb8a5ea3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "594f1c20-7761-5fb2-8775-ffe6b852f1ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-94999" :question/problem "Given that $h(x,y)={x}^{2}y,$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $h(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & ={r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r} & =(2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ee45610-e14c-593e-9002-e2b2fcdb4517" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8de20450-8e75-5421-a8fd-9f931881b4dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r^{2}\\sin^{2} \\theta  + 2rz\\cos \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124007} {:answer/id #uuid "b8cb917e-c20f-5a58-879d-027b5d61c72b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\sin \\theta  + z^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124008} {:answer/id #uuid "bdfabaff-d294-5863-9a4e-b1d9a9ccc3f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cos \\theta  + 2rz\\sin \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124009} {:answer/id #uuid "ff28c095-8df6-523b-862d-4d69a164be9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r^{2}\\sin (2\\theta ) + rz^{2}\\cos \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124010} {:answer/id #uuid "441edfd6-d4c5-5918-88d6-0b2390d3070d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r^{2}\\sin \\theta  + 2r\\cos \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8de20450-8e75-5421-a8fd-9f931881b4dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r^{2}\\sin^{2} \\theta  + 2rz\\cos \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124007} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec54e909-ce31-5cb3-bf5b-33d69276af29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08bad6ab-1a42-5f7a-8850-36801983c246" :question/math-academy-id "q-95047" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=xy+{y}^{2}z+x{z}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂z},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂z}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂z}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}+2xz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z} & ={r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366761 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eec8b132-fe60-5c11-bc33-8e91d76eb08a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366763} {:answer/id #uuid "0f996c5a-5c2f-5997-8b17-ef558e0a7e96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366764} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca25f34-88c4-5378-8b24-9ae7387e28cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{r}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366765} {:answer/id #uuid "80bd4c7d-a356-5d7c-89dc-752d0bebb66e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366766} {:answer/id #uuid "94a49d55-fca1-5a93-8cff-4b63b3e1ccd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f996c5a-5c2f-5997-8b17-ef558e0a7e96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366764} :answer-field/id #uuid "de7c7ed0-f35a-5114-b024-a303e4e85237" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b70a410a-0ccc-550e-9162-f7191fd8a677" :question/math-academy-id "q-95109" :question/problem "Given that $w(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =\\frac{2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }, \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r} & =(\\frac{2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2r({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366768 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53b44139-4d0d-5beb-b687-2cffadcf974d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366770} {:answer/id #uuid "12a4332f-09b3-5719-ba56-b15fd152c877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366771} {:answer/id #uuid "89941e85-afab-5070-8383-36ca14059ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366772} {:answer/id #uuid "8c914b38-6a39-584e-97db-f706718c4b7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366773} {:answer/id #uuid "6fce807e-e1ea-53cd-85b1-3ad7cd43c8dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89941e85-afab-5070-8383-36ca14059ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366772} :answer-field/id #uuid "deb5ed78-a5c0-5c11-bf82-40ec095da0eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ae2c7b7-527a-5ca1-b6af-3288664a7337" :question/math-academy-id "q-95112" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +0\\,(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =2r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2973c3d-8b10-56fc-9f70-3490d2a6e473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366777} {:answer/id #uuid "50c0ec90-99dc-51f4-9f34-8d5a10cfa647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366778} {:answer/id #uuid "bad05929-16d4-514b-9055-fd28e581e4bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366779} {:answer/id #uuid "d373aa96-6923-537d-91eb-6a3060976f5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366780} {:answer/id #uuid "e06d5d52-2baa-5f6f-868a-a73576f32613" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d373aa96-6923-537d-91eb-6a3060976f5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366780} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab5a91ad-0d26-55ab-a7ab-e6ec062436c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8bbd597d-8a52-5b49-bca0-d5f1bc57cdee" :question/math-academy-id "q-95113" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)={y}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $g(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂g}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂g}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0, \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta } & =0\\,(-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366782 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0a85912-5cc2-5a02-9863-a855fb9e2528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366784} {:answer/id #uuid "f877186f-1582-507c-a12c-49bfde56830d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366785} {:answer/id #uuid "b208e69d-7d14-5520-9604-43dc05e042a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366786} {:answer/id #uuid "3a1a6918-6b16-5ec6-9cee-25708f384c94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366787} {:answer/id #uuid "9a900799-c808-5c78-914f-7063acd0dc9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a900799-c808-5c78-914f-7063acd0dc9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366788} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2fa1259-1b11-5fbc-915a-ebdcafd671cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0596bdf9-030b-5ecb-a526-ecd7e37376f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-95116" :question/problem "Given that $w(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2},$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂w}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂w}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=x,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dfb4b64-2dd9-50d5-aa06-67e772ee7c5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366791} {:answer/id #uuid "e98b42ae-d5aa-58cc-90f8-9919ef056bf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366792} {:answer/id #uuid "3f531d64-4604-5458-88ef-49441bb5747e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366793} {:answer/id #uuid "5afbf091-88d9-54d1-a5b5-ead29c42b9d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366794} {:answer/id #uuid "858d89e6-a6a6-532e-b3e4-ddd836d0ed1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dfb4b64-2dd9-50d5-aa06-67e772ee7c5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366791} :answer-field/id #uuid "a010313b-a155-56ac-89d7-bc5139671cad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a4c3679-da19-53bb-b6aa-185a2b1676ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-95129" :question/problem "Given that $h(r,\\theta )=2r+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=\\frac{∂h}{∂r}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r}=2,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =(2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366796 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61b8247d-9dc6-5960-bcd1-5afe0ec8692b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366798} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c1d189-c84b-5bd4-b271-6c652461f32b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366799} {:answer/id #uuid "57e356b3-adda-5639-b709-4fb1d1badd8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366800} {:answer/id #uuid "c58f46f3-405b-5214-a2af-c08da59c212a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366801} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc85a01-c966-5084-b160-fb4923ce4adb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cc85a01-c966-5084-b160-fb4923ce4adb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366802} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aebf7c1-aeca-525c-b590-8cfee3d311ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7bb8b7d1-721d-5364-bbb6-b889dc2e4427" :question/math-academy-id "q-95130" :question/problem "Given that $g(r,\\theta )={r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta ,$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂y},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{∂g}{∂r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂r}=2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta }=-3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}(-3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =r(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366803 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e58038de-9b8e-5a70-8001-2fb4b350011f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366805} {:answer/id #uuid "fa8274f0-fef6-528c-af17-fcde32543624" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366806} {:answer/id #uuid "66ec02ce-3590-5bfa-a765-807ed1d4fec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366807} {:answer/id #uuid "f100962d-738f-5819-bb53-0e33162a35b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366808} {:answer/id #uuid "955ab586-c169-5580-a564-9fa7c1198b82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e58038de-9b8e-5a70-8001-2fb4b350011f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366805} :answer-field/id #uuid "aac74885-768a-5c63-9b45-c30476a54d1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "476388b4-86c1-51ee-8706-4567adb08b6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95132" :question/problem "Given that $f(r,\\theta )=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂r}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta }=2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21482878-d381-52e6-bc99-0643d03350ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366812} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ca6ed8-a801-5c05-8eba-ffdf840d966e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366813} {:answer/id #uuid "75705233-7691-5516-97a0-9bbbe4e0ae0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366814} {:answer/id #uuid "5e4cc256-8a69-5f53-b73b-434521e5f509" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366815} {:answer/id #uuid "71c8e706-6497-5d4a-b541-59e1c3080288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9ca6ed8-a801-5c05-8eba-ffdf840d966e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366813} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0e9344f-b8e9-51ee-96cc-0e80dd1e7b92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0bae42a2-4697-5514-b36b-4e2bedcb4a24" :question/math-academy-id "q-95133" :question/problem "Given that $w(r,\\theta )=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ,$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂y},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=\\frac{∂w}{∂r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta }=-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}(-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ee45610-e14c-593e-9002-e2b2fcdb4517" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5da02696-43c8-5366-8912-5c15aecb01bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{r}^{2}(z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366819} {:answer/id #uuid "dcfbc841-cb04-5009-8e66-ba8294c38ec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(2z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366820} {:answer/id #uuid "fa2a8993-cc05-530a-af04-4b9260382db4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(2z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366821} {:answer/id #uuid "a505e4d0-2e18-527c-b03f-d9b1a66a03aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366822} {:answer/id #uuid "3c192e27-55e1-5c4a-8528-3f71de0cadf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcfbc841-cb04-5009-8e66-ba8294c38ec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(2z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366820} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2ce4d48-ca95-55c9-889f-d19d59e19df0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43721544-cb6f-5228-b23a-f001474e0bc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95171" :question/problem "Given that $u(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xyz,$ find $\\frac{∂u}{∂z},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $u(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂u}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂z}+\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂z}+\\frac{∂u}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂u}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $u$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂z}=2{x}^{2}z+xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =2{r}^{2}z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}(2z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ee45610-e14c-593e-9002-e2b2fcdb4517" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d51283a-dce3-555a-a741-802628ca118c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +2r{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366826} {:answer/id #uuid "3477523f-8d71-5d42-be2e-a7f9639a839d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +z\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366827} {:answer/id #uuid "f56d9167-f153-5b24-8299-31b769e8fd24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366828} {:answer/id #uuid "2b543ee1-8f19-554a-840f-7cc48f417948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366829} {:answer/id #uuid "e20ea708-4697-5ede-8499-406bfb0b40b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "rz\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e20ea708-4697-5ede-8499-406bfb0b40b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "rz\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366830} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd9ef781-8dfc-5a31-a251-fd8818b850e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "920693d5-b134-58f3-9ce3-1cf90cc1bd7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95172" :question/problem "Given that $v(x,y,z)=2y-xz,$ find $\\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $v(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂v}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂v}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂v}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂v}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $v$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂x}=-z,\\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-z, \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta } & =-(-z)r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2)r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =rz\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366831 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ee45610-e14c-593e-9002-e2b2fcdb4517" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e38d6de-f827-5523-a866-6654a6eecdf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8z+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366833} {:answer/id #uuid "2eed0580-6778-503d-8ef0-a31ab03ea018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{r}^{2}z{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366834} {:answer/id #uuid "c7095808-2526-5b50-b218-c1b66ffd4d66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8z+{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366835} {:answer/id #uuid "c70da6f3-1e3a-5d14-a32e-97bdfb876720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366836} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6ec0f8-f1f0-5b7b-9ad6-3b89f8e1a2c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{r}^{2}z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec6ec0f8-f1f0-5b7b-9ad6-3b89f8e1a2c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{r}^{2}z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366837} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cadad47-2fb5-5ef0-811b-88272c04b4db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7a42087-ded8-51f0-a5cc-20cabf1e3176" :question/math-academy-id "q-95174" :question/problem "Given that $h(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2}{z}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂z},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $h(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂z}+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂z}+\\frac{∂h}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂h}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂z}=8{x}^{2}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂z} & =8{r}^{2}z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
